# Cloudflare Pages response headers (copied verbatim to out/_headers on export).
# The OG image routes are extension-less files, so static hosting would serve
# them as octet-stream and social scrapers would reject them. Force image/png.
/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/hand/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
# The route handler's Content-Type is dropped by the static export (no server
# is left to send it), so Pages would serve the feed off the .xml extension as
# application/xml. Readers accept that, but say what it actually is.
/rss.xml
  Content-Type: application/rss+xml; charset=utf-8
# The Pages project's production alias serves this same build on a second host,
# and Cloudflare only adds noindex to *preview* deployments, so
# pip-web-9oj.pages.dev is a fully crawlable duplicate of the whole site. The
# canonical tag on every page points at playpip.io, but a canonical is a hint
# and a directive is not, and the one thing we cannot afford on a domain this
# young is splitting what little ranking signal exists across two hosts.
#
# A rule with a host in it applies to that host only, so playpip.io is
# untouched. Preview deployments are a different host again
# (<hash>.pip-web-9oj.pages.dev) and keep the noindex Cloudflare gives them,
# which matters because their permanent addresses are how any past build gets
# audited from outside (see the account outage, #92).
#
# Chosen over a 301 in a Pages Function: a root functions/_middleware.ts runs
# for every request to the project including static assets, so redirecting one
# host would put a Worker invocation in front of every chunk, image and font on
# playpip.io as well. That is a real cost for an SEO hygiene fix, and this file
# costs nothing at runtime.
https://pip-web-9oj.pages.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

